<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Cover Letter - Matillion Senior AI Engineering Manager</title>
  <style>
    @page { size: A4; margin: 16mm 18mm; }
    html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; }
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 10.5pt; line-height: 1.34; color: #111; }
    p { margin: 0 0 9px 0; text-align: justify; text-justify: inter-word; hyphens: auto; }
    .contact { margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .contact p, .date, .salutation, .signoff { text-align: left; }
    .date { margin-bottom: 14px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="contact">
    <p>Christos Hadjinikolis<br>
    christos.hadjinikolis@gmail.com<br>
    https://christos-hadjinikolis.github.io/<br>
    https://uk.linkedin.com/in/christoshadjinikolis</p>
  </div>

  <p class="date">1 August 2026</p>

  <p class="salutation">Dear Matillion hiring team,</p>

  <p>I am applying for the Senior AI Engineering Manager role because Maia Core sounds like the kind of platform problem I have spent my career moving towards: an engineering team responsible for the harness, services, operating model, and reliability disciplines that let AI/data capabilities become dependable product infrastructure.</p>

  <p>At Vortexa, I own the engineering strategy and delivery for a client-facing ML and streaming estate that processes roughly 6 million records per hour into production intelligence. I lead a 10-person cross-functional team across engineering, Product, and domain expertise, directly manage the MLE/DE/DS core, and helped grow the wider Data Production Team from 4 to 30+ people through hiring loops, system-design interviews, onboarding, mentoring, and engineering practices.</p>

  <p>The closest analogue to Maia Core's architectural inflection point is the two-year Kafka Streams-to-Flink migration I led for critical live pipelines. That work was not a framework preference; it was a platform evolution to reduce partition-coupled scaling, improve state/checkpointing, increase operational visibility, and lower maintenance effort while keeping delivery moving.</p>

  <p>I have also built the trust machinery around production ML: MLflow, model and data versioning, service-based deployment, replay and evaluation interfaces, live accuracy monitoring, observability, runbooks, rollback and fallback paths, Jira alert workflows, and shared on-call ownership. Those are the same engineering concerns I would expect to matter in an agentic platform: outputs need to be evaluated, services need to be observable, failures need controlled recovery paths, and teams need clear standards for what is safe to ship.</p>

  <p>Alongside that production role, I have spent the last six months building Promet, a private local-first AI harness shell/workbench for memory-aware, voice-enabled, tool-using assistants. I use it as hands-on platform judgement rather than a scale claim: orchestration, local runtimes, tool execution, prompt contracts, MCP-style connectors, approvals, traceability, replay, and the user experience around safe delegation.</p>

  <p>I am comfortable across Java/JVM-oriented streaming systems and Python-heavy ML/data workflows, and I care about the practical interface between platform teams and their internal users. The value I would bring to Matillion is an engineering manager who stays close to the technical substance, builds strong delivery and service ownership habits, and turns research-heavy AI/data work into reliable platforms that product teams can trust and build on.</p>

  <p class="signoff">Sincerely,</p>

  <p class="signoff">Christos Hadjinikolis</p>
</body>
</html>
